india is becoming the new epicenter of  the pandemic the country posted a new  single day  world record reporting more than 332  thousand new covet cases this morning  the actual number is likely to be much  higher  and as lucy kraft reports the country  with the world's second largest  population  is desperate for help toward to ward off  a catastrophe even mass cremations held  at makeshift facilities  can't keep up with the pace of death as  covid cases explode across india  workers say they've never seen anything  like it with even young children among  the victims  families gathered nearby said their  final farewells  nobody helped in time we were running  here and there for a ventilator  she died after the oxygen ran out said  this man whose mother was among the dead  about half of india states are in  lockdown  the country's health care system is  collapsing with widespread shortages of  medicine  oxygen ventilators and hospital beds  said independent journalist smita sharma  i think there's just one way to describe  it right now it's a  living hell india once praised for its  handling of the initial coveted wave  last  year is now being criticized for opening  up too soon  and for mixed messaging prime minister  narendra modi who this week said india  had been hit by a coveted storm  has been rebuked for allowing mass  political rallies to continue  often with lacks antiviral measures  and a traditional hindu bathing festival  the kumaila  was permitted this year drawing millions  of pilgrims  it's now been deemed a super spreader  event dr abhishek rimal said virus  mutations and pandemic fatigue  could make india the world's worst  coveted hot spot in the current pace  if we really do not put a major public  health measure to bring this down  it will definitely become the new  epicenter in days to come  vaccinations are running behind schedule  and even before this current wave peaks  experts are bracing for a third wave  that could further strain the country's  health care system  anthony lucy kraft in tokyo thank you
 

